cff-version: 1.2.0
message: >-
  If you use this software, please cite both this repository and the original
  MuLAN paper listed under `references`.
title: "MuLAN fork: leakage-controlled PLM + FoldX evaluation for protein-protein binding ddG"
abstract: >-
  A research fork of MuLAN extending it with multiple protein-language-model
  embedding backends, a FoldX binding-ddG augmentation channel, and
  leakage-controlled evaluation protocols (by-complex, sequence-clustered, and
  CATH-superfamily hold-out) scored with per-structure Spearman correlation for
  direct comparison with the published ddG frontier.
type: software
authors:
  - family-names: Chin
    given-names: Connor
    email: cchin29@stanford.edu
    affiliation: Stanford University
version: "0.1.0"
date-released: "2026-09-12"
license: CC-BY-NC-SA-4.0
license-url: "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/"
url: "https://github.com/cchin29/mulan-plm-foldx"
repository-code: "https://github.com/cchin29/mulan-plm-foldx"
# `version` tracks pyproject.toml and must be bumped with it -- a CITATION.cff that names a
# different version than the package is worse than one that names none.
#
# `date-released` matches the tag it was written in; bump it with `version`. Add a Zenodo DOI
# under `identifiers:` in the same pass if one is minted.
keywords:
  - protein-protein interaction
  - binding free energy
  - ddG
  - SKEMPI
  - protein language model
  - FoldX
  - data leakage
  - light attention
references:
  - type: article
    title: >-
      MuLAN: Mutation-driven Light Attention Networks for investigating
      protein-protein interactions from sequences
    authors:
      - family-names: Lombardi
        given-names: Gianluca
      - family-names: Carbone
        given-names: Alessandra
    year: 2024
    doi: 10.1101/2024.08.24.609515
    journal: bioRxiv
    notes: >-
      Upstream work that this repository forks. Canonical at
      https://gitlab.lcqb.upmc.fr/lombardi/mulan, mirrored at
      https://github.com/GianLMB/mulan.

  # Every ddG label in this repository. SKEMPI 2.0 is CC BY 4.0, so attribution is a licence
  # term, not a courtesy; NOTICE states which files adapt it and how.
  - type: article
    title: >-
      SKEMPI 2.0: an updated benchmark of changes in protein–protein binding energy,
      kinetics and thermodynamics upon mutation
    authors:
      - family-names: Jankauskaitė
        given-names: Justina
      - family-names: Jiménez-García
        given-names: Brian
      - family-names: Dapkūnas
        given-names: Justas
      - family-names: Fernández-Recio
        given-names: Juan
      - family-names: Moal
        given-names: Iain H.
    year: 2019
    doi: 10.1093/bioinformatics/bty635
    journal: Bioinformatics
    volume: 35
    issue: 3
    start: 462
    end: 469

  # The program behind the FoldX channel, which is what every arm above `base` reads. The
  # producing pipeline lives in skempi-foldx; the computed outputs ship here.
  - type: article
    title: "FoldX 5.0: working with RNA, small molecules and a new graphical interface"
    authors:
      - family-names: Delgado
        given-names: Javier
      - family-names: Radusky
        given-names: Leandro G.
      - family-names: Cianferoni
        given-names: Damiano
      - family-names: Serrano
        given-names: Luis
    year: 2019
    doi: 10.1093/bioinformatics/btz184
    journal: Bioinformatics
    volume: 35
    issue: 20
    start: 4168
    end: 4169

  # The CATH-superfamily hold-out is the primary evaluation tier here, and the partition is not
  # ours: it is a join onto the static `cath_fold` column USP-ddG ships, following the protocol
  # CATH-ddG introduced. Which of the two first constructed it cannot be established from either
  # repository, so both are cited and neither is claimed as sole originator. See NOTICE.
  - type: article
    title: >-
      CATH-ddG: towards robust mutation effect prediction on protein–protein interactions
      out of CATH homologous superfamily
    authors:
      - family-names: Yu
        given-names: Guanglei
      - family-names: Bi
        given-names: Xuehua
      - family-names: Ma
        given-names: Teng
      - family-names: Li
        given-names: Yaohang
      - family-names: Wang
        given-names: Jianxin
    year: 2025
    doi: 10.1093/bioinformatics/btaf228
    journal: Bioinformatics
    volume: 41
    issue: Supplement_1
    start: i362
    end: i372
  - type: article
    title: >-
      USP-ddG: a unified structural paradigm with data efficacy and mixture-of-experts for
      predicting mutational effects on protein–protein interactions
    authors:
      - family-names: Yu
        given-names: Guanglei
      - family-names: Bi
        given-names: Xuehua
      - family-names: Zhao
        given-names: Qichang
      - family-names: Wang
        given-names: Jianxin
    year: 2026
    doi: 10.1093/bioinformatics/btag249
    journal: Bioinformatics
    volume: 42
    issue: Supplement_1
    notes: >-
      Ships the `cath_fold` column the CATH partition is joined from. The journal version;
      it was preprinted as bioRxiv 10.1101/2025.11.09.687124.

  # The classification underneath that partition. CC BY 4.0, which carries a citation requirement.
  - type: article
    title: "CATH: increased structural coverage of functional space"
    authors:
      - family-names: Sillitoe
        given-names: Ian
      - family-names: Bordin
        given-names: Nicola
      - family-names: Dawson
        given-names: Natalie
      - family-names: Orengo
        given-names: Christine A.
    year: 2021
    doi: 10.1093/nar/gkaa1079
    journal: Nucleic Acids Research
    volume: 49
    issue: D1
    start: D266
    end: D273
    notes: "First three authors plus the senior author; see the paper for the full list."

  # Supplies the PDB-chain to CATH-superfamily mapping behind the leakage analysis. CC BY 4.0.
  - type: article
    title: >-
      SIFTS: updated Structure Integration with Function, Taxonomy and Sequences resource
      allows 40-fold increase in coverage of structure-based annotations for proteins
    authors:
      - family-names: Dana
        given-names: Jose M.
      - family-names: Gutmanas
        given-names: Aleksandras
      - family-names: Tyagi
        given-names: Nidhi
      - family-names: Velankar
        given-names: Sameer
    year: 2019
    doi: 10.1093/nar/gky1114
    journal: Nucleic Acids Research
    volume: 47
    issue: D1
    start: D482
    end: D489
    notes: "First three authors plus the senior author; see the paper for the full list."

  # Not dependencies, but they set the numbers: the S4169 selection fixed the per-complex
  # mutation lists for 2,494 of the 4,238 shipped single-point FoldX records, and a FoldX ddG
  # depends on every entry before it in the list it was computed with. S4169 is **mCSM-PPI2's**
  # selection -- GeoPPI's own paper attributes it to Rodrigues et al., and the two files agree
  # row for row on all 4,169 PDB identifiers and all 4,169 ddG values. GeoPPI follows it as the
  # copy these lists were taken from, since its renumbering is what the `_source` labels record.
  - type: article
    title: "mCSM-PPI2: predicting the effects of mutations on protein–protein interactions"
    authors:
      - family-names: Rodrigues
        given-names: Carlos H. M.
      - family-names: Myung
        given-names: Yoochan
      - family-names: Pires
        given-names: Douglas E. V.
      - family-names: Ascher
        given-names: David B.
    year: 2019
    doi: 10.1093/nar/gkz383
    journal: Nucleic Acids Research
    volume: 47
    issue: W1
    start: W338
    end: W344
    notes: >-
      Origin of the S4169 selection. Article CC BY 4.0; the dataset page is marked Open
      Knowledge.
  - type: article
    title: >-
      Deep geometric representations for modeling effects of mutations on protein-protein
      binding affinity
    authors:
      - family-names: Liu
        given-names: Xianggen
      - family-names: Luo
        given-names: Yunan
      - family-names: Li
        given-names: Pengyong
      - family-names: Song
        given-names: Sen
      - family-names: Peng
        given-names: Jian
    year: 2021
    doi: 10.1371/journal.pcbi.1009284
    journal: PLOS Computational Biology
    volume: 17
    issue: 8
    start: e1009284
    notes: >-
      The copy S4169 was obtained from (MIT licensed). It renumbered residues and renamed
      columns; the row set and the ddG values are mCSM-PPI2's, unchanged.

  # Built the <=60% sequence-identity clustered splits, which is one of the three leakage-control
  # protocols the headline numbers are reported under.
  - type: article
    title: >-
      MMseqs2 enables sensitive protein sequence searching for the analysis of massive
      data sets
    authors:
      - family-names: Steinegger
        given-names: Martin
      - family-names: Söding
        given-names: Johannes
    year: 2017
    doi: 10.1038/nbt.3988
    journal: Nature Biotechnology
    volume: 35
    issue: 11
    start: 1026
    end: 1028

  # The structures the FoldX channel was computed on. Not redistributed here; the outputs are.
  - type: article
    title: "The Protein Data Bank"
    authors:
      - family-names: Berman
        given-names: Helen M.
      - family-names: Westbrook
        given-names: John
      - family-names: Feng
        given-names: Zukang
      - family-names: Bourne
        given-names: Philip E.
    year: 2000
    doi: 10.1093/nar/28.1.235
    journal: Nucleic Acids Research
    volume: 28
    issue: 1
    start: 235
    end: 242
    notes: "First three authors plus the senior author; see the paper for the full list."

  # The predicted structures the per-residue structural context is computed from. Not
  # redistributed, but values derived from them are (experiments/structctx_sweep/results/*.json),
  # and AFDB is CC BY 4.0 -- so this is a licence term, like SKEMPI and CATH above. AlphaFold DB
  # asks for the method paper and the database paper separately; both are here.
  - type: article
    title: "Highly accurate protein structure prediction with AlphaFold"
    authors:
      - family-names: Jumper
        given-names: John
      - family-names: Evans
        given-names: Richard
      - family-names: Pritzel
        given-names: Alexander
      - family-names: Hassabis
        given-names: Demis
    year: 2021
    doi: 10.1038/s41586-021-03819-2
    journal: Nature
    volume: 596
    start: 583
    end: 589
    notes: "First three authors plus the senior author; see the paper for the full list."
  - type: article
    title: >-
      AlphaFold Protein Structure Database 2025: a redesigned interface and updated
      structural coverage
    authors:
      - family-names: Bertoni
        given-names: Damian
      - family-names: Tsenkov
        given-names: Maxim
      - family-names: Magana
        given-names: Paulyna
      - family-names: Velankar
        given-names: Sameer
    year: 2025
    doi: 10.1093/nar/gkaf1226
    journal: Nucleic Acids Research
    volume: 54
    issue: D1
    start: D358
    end: D362
    notes: >-
      The database scripts/uniprot_visualize.py queries, and the paper AlphaFold DB currently
      asks users to cite. It supersedes the 2024 database paper (10.1093/nar/gkad1011).
      First three authors plus the senior author.

  # Produces the secondary structure and the absolute solvent accessibility that the RSA values
  # in experiments/structctx_sweep/results/*.json are computed from.
  - type: article
    title: >-
      Dictionary of protein secondary structure: pattern recognition of hydrogen-bonded
      and geometrical features
    authors:
      - family-names: Kabsch
        given-names: Wolfgang
      - family-names: Sander
        given-names: Christian
    year: 1983
    doi: 10.1002/bip.360221211
    journal: Biopolymers
    volume: 22
    issue: 12
    start: 2577
    end: 2637

# The published comparators in the README leaderboard (CATH-ddG, USP-ddG, flex-ddG, BA-DDG,
# Prompt-DDG, RDE-Network, DiffAffinity, PPIformer, ProtBFF) are transcribed in
# data/benchmarks/frontier.tsv with a per-row source, which is where they belong -- they are
# numbers this work is measured against, not components of it. The PLM backbones are likewise
# runtime choices rather than components; scripts_plots/results_matrix.py maps each tag to its
# model.
